-- title:
--   Non-constant rational function on the generic fibre of a Čerednik–Drinfeld quotient
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $r$. The base data consist of a characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O$ that is a discrete valuation ring, an element $\pi$ of $\mathcal O$ assumed irreducible (`hπ`), the hypothesis that $\mathcal O$ is complete for the $\pi$-adic filtration (`hcomplete`), the hypothesis `hres` that the residue ring $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ has exactly $r$ elements, the hypothesis `hunr` that the ideals generated by the image of $r$ and by $\pi$ coincide (absolute unramifiedness), and a characteristic-zero field $K_0$ that is a fraction field of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   The unramified coefficient ring is a characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O$-algebra $O^{nr}$ together with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $Fr$, subject to: completeness of $O^{nr}$ for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`); maximality of that ideal (`hOnr_max`); `hOnr_alg`, that every element of $O^{nr}$ satisfies, modulo that ideal, some monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$; `hOnr_closed`, that every monic polynomial over $O^{nr}$ of positive degree has a root modulo that ideal; and `hFr`, that $Fr(x) \equiv x^{r}$ modulo that ideal for all $x$.
--
--   A group homomorphism $vdet : \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0) \to \mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) is given, characterised by `hvdet`: $vdet(g)$ equals $n$ precisely when $\det g = u\pi^{n}$ in $K_0$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal O$; so $vdet$ is the $\pi$-adic valuation of the determinant.
--
--   The group datum consists of a group $G$, a homomorphism $\sigma : G \to \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$, a subgroup $\Gamma \le G$, and: `hcent`, the existence of $z \in \Gamma$ with $\sigma z$ a scalar matrix $c \cdot 1$ and $vdet(\sigma z) = 2$; `hodd`, the existence of $w \in \Gamma$ with $vdet(\sigma w) = 1$; a subgroup $\Gamma'$ characterised by `hΓ'` as the set of $x \in \Gamma$ with $vdet(\sigma x)$ even; and a homomorphism $\rho : G \to \mathrm{PGL}_2(K_0)$ with `hρ` asserting that $\rho g$ is the class of $\sigma g$. Three conditions are imposed on the action on the Bruhat–Tits tree of full $\mathcal O$-lattices in $K_0^2$ up to homothety: `hvtf`, the existence of a subgroup $\Gamma'' \le \Gamma'$ of non-zero relative index in $\Gamma'$ all of whose elements of finite order in $\rho(\Gamma'')$ are trivial (virtual torsion-freeness); `hdisc`, finiteness of $\{g \in \rho(\Gamma') : g \cdot v = v\}$ for every vertex $v$; and `hcocpt`, the existence of a finite set $S$ of vertices meeting every $\rho(\Gamma')$-orbit.
--
--   On the algebro-geometric side there is a scheme $\mathcal X$ and a proper flat morphism $f : \mathcal X \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, together with a family $\Theta$ which, for each $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which the image of $\pi$ is nilpotent, sends a pair consisting of an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $O^{nr} \to B$ and a Deligne datum over $B$ (an assignment, to each full lattice $M \subset K_0^2$, of a $B$-submodule $\mathrm{line}(M)$ of $B \otimes_{\mathcal O} M$ with invertible quotient, compatible with lattice inclusions, equivariant for scalar homotheties, and non-degenerate at every prime ideal of $B$ in the sense of the `nondeg` clause) to a morphism $\operatorname{Spec} B \to \mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$. Four properties of $\Theta$ are assumed: `hΘnat`, naturality in $B$ along $\mathcal O$-algebra maps; `hΘinv`, invariance under $\Gamma$, i.e. $\Theta(x') = \Theta(x)$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma$ and `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct` holds for $\sigma\gamma$, which means that the $O^{nr}$-component of $x'$ is that of $x$ precomposed with $Fr^{-vdet(\sigma\gamma)}$ and that the Deligne datum of $x'$ is the pullback of that of $x$ along $(\sigma\gamma)^{-1}$; `hΘfib`, that for every algebraically closed field $k$ which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra with nilpotent image of $\pi$ and every $\psi : O^{nr} \to k$, the map $P \mapsto \Theta(\psi, P)$ is onto the $k$-points of $\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and its fibres are exactly the orbits of the twisted $\Gamma$-action; and `hΘuniv`, the universal property that any family $\rho'$ of maps into the nilpotent-$\pi$ points of another $\mathcal O$-scheme $T$ which is natural and $\Gamma$-invariant factors as $u \circ \Theta$ for a natural family $u$, unique among natural families with that factorisation.
--
--   The analytic datum comprises: an algebraically closed field $C$, extension of $K_0$, equipped with a valuation with values in a linearly ordered commutative group with zero $\Gamma_0$, complete for that valuation; a commutative ring $R$ that is an $\mathcal O$-algebra mapping to $C$, with the evident scalar towers; a pseudo-uniformiser $\varpi$, that is an element of $K_0$ whose image in $C$ has valuation strictly between $0$ and $1$ and such that the valuation of any non-zero element of $K_0$ is squeezed between a power of $v(\varpi)$ and the corresponding inverse power; the hypothesis `hF` that $(\pi, \varpi, R)$ is an adic frame, i.e. $\pi$ is irreducible, $R \to C$ is injective with image exactly the unit ball $\{v \le 1\}$, $R$ is complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$, the elements of $K_0$ of valuation at most $1$ in $C$ are exactly those coming from $\mathcal O$, the image of $K_0$ in $C$ is closed, and the images of $\pi$ and $\varpi$ in $C$ agree; an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi_0 : O^{nr} \to R$; `hrk`, that for $x, y \in C$ with $v(x) < 1$ and $y \ne 0$ some power $v(x)^n$ is at most $v(y)$; and `hval`, that every non-zero $\varepsilon \in \Gamma_0$ dominates the valuation of some non-zero element of $C$. Further, `htame` asserts that for every vertex $v$ of the tree the cardinality of the stabiliser $\{g \in \rho(\Gamma') : g\cdot v = v\}$, viewed in $C$, has valuation $1$; `hex` asserts that $\varpi$ is exhausting, i.e. every point of the Drinfeld upper half plane $C \setminus K_0$ lies in one of the affinoids $\mathrm{affinoid}\,\varpi\,n$; and the ring of functions on the upper half plane that are holomorphic on each of these affinoids is assumed to be a domain.
--
--   The uniformisation is a map $\Phi$ from adic points — compatible systems of Deligne data over the quotients $R/(\pi^{n+1})$ — to the set of morphisms $p : \operatorname{Spec} R \to \mathcal X$ with $p$ followed by $f$ equal to the structural morphism $\operatorname{Spec} R \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, subject to `hΦ`, a conjunction of four clauses: the reduction of $\Phi x$ modulo $\pi^{n+1}$ is $\Theta$ evaluated at the pair formed by $\psi_0$ reduced modulo $\pi^{n+1}$ and the $n$-th component of $x$, for all $x$ and $n$; $\Phi$ is surjective; $\Phi x = \Phi x'$ holds if and only if $x' = x \cdot \sigma\gamma$ for some $\gamma \in \Gamma'$; and base change along $R \to C$ is injective on these $R$-points and hits every morphism $\operatorname{Spec} C \to \mathcal X$ lying over the structural morphism $\operatorname{Spec} C \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$.
--
--   Finally, $s_C : \operatorname{Spec} C \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ is the morphism induced by the composite $\mathcal O \to K_0 \to C$ (this is the content of `hsC`), and the pullback $\mathcal X_C$ of $f$ along $s_C$ is assumed integral.
--
--   Conclusion: there exists an element $s$ of the function field of $\mathcal X_C$ such that for every $c \in C$ one has $s \ne \mathrm{baseToFunctionField}(\mathrm{pullback.snd}\, f\, s_C)(c)$, where [`AlgebraicCurve.baseToFunctionField`](def/AlgebraicCurve_CurveModel.html#L18) applied to the projection $\mathcal X_C \to \operatorname{Spec} C$ is the ring homomorphism $C \to K(\mathcal X_C)$ obtained from the global-sections map of that projection followed by the germ at the generic point. In other words, the function field of the generic fibre is not exhausted by the constants.
--
--   This is the non-constancy step in the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation of the generic fibre: from the adic uniformisation $\Phi$ of the $R$-points of $\mathcal X$ by $\Gamma'$-orbits of Deligne data one produces a rational function on $\mathcal X_C$ outside the image of $C$. It is obtained from the existence of a point of $\mathcal X_C$ other than the generic point ([`CerednikDrinfeld.exists_ne_genericPoint_pullback_of_cerednikDrinfeld_quotient`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.exists_ne_genericPoint_pullback_of_cerednikDrinfeld_quotient)) together with the general fact about integral schemes [`AlgebraicGeometry.exists_functionField_ne_baseToFunctionField_of_ne_genericPoint`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.exists_functionField_ne_baseToFunctionField_of_ne_genericPoint), and is used in the proof that the function field of $\mathcal X_C$ maps onto the invariant field of the ring of holomorphic functions on the Drinfeld upper half plane.
